From: sKyissKy <29267720+sKyissKy@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] zerotier: add three-tier cryptographic acceleration hierarchy (CPU AES -> EIP93 -> Salsa20)

Implement an automatic three-tier cryptographic fallback hierarchy for ZeroTier One:

1. Tier 1 (Native CPU AES):
   - Prioritize hardware AES instructions (x86 AES-NI or ARMv8 Crypto Extensions).
   - Zero syscall overhead; runs in user-space CPU registers (e.g. Airoha AN7583,
     MediaTek MT7981 / MT7986, Rockchip RK3588, x86).

2. Tier 2 (EIP-93 Hardware Coprocessor via Dynamic Kernel Probe):
   - For CPUs lacking hardware AES instructions, dynamically probe whether the Linux
     kernel has an active SafeXcel EIP-93 skcipher driver ("ctr(aes-eip93)") via AF_ALG.
   - Eliminates hardcoded device-tree SoC whitelists, auto-detecting any SoC with EIP-93
     (e.g. Airoha AN7581 / EN7581).
   - Reuses persistent AF_ALG operational sockets per CTR session; bounds payload
     threshold (>= 256 bytes) to eliminate syscall overhead on tiny packets.
   - Allows explicit runtime opt-out via ZEROTIER_EIP93=off environment variable.

3. Tier 3 (Automatic Salsa20 Fallback for Pure Software Devices):
   - For devices with neither CPU AES nor EIP-93 (e.g. MediaTek MT7621 MIPS,
     legacy ARMv7, and non-crypto MIPS architectures), advertise protocol version 11
     in HELLO/OK packets and suppress AES key export.
   - Peers automatically negotiate Curve25519 + Salsa20/12 + Poly1305, achieving
     200-300+ Mbps throughput on general-purpose registers without modifying wire protocols.

Based on EIP-93 hardware acceleration work by coolsnowwolf:
https://github.com/fw876/helloworld/commit/9213963c6a798658c55c7402f3ae7db81d891256

Co-authored-by: coolsnowwolf <coolsnowwolf@gmail.com>
Signed-off-by: sKyissKy <29267720+sKyissKy@users.noreply.github.com>
